The four, verified:

```js
// Promise.all — fail-fast, order preserved
const r = await Promise.all([p(1), p(2), p(3)]);  // [1, 2, 3] ✓
// if p(2) rejects → all rejects immediately with that error,
// and p(1), p(3) results are DISCARDED (they still run)

// allSettled — never rejects; you inspect each outcome
const s = await Promise.allSettled([p(1), fail('x'), p(3)]);
// [{status:'fulfilled',value:1}, {status:'rejected',reason:x}, {status:'fulfilled',value:3}] ✓

// race — first to settle wins, whatever it is
Promise.race([slow(50ms), p('fast')]);  // 'fast' ✓
// the timeout pattern: race([fetch(), timeoutPromise]) — timeout rejects → you bail

// any — first FULFILLMENT wins; rejections ignored
// Promise.any([fail(a), p('B'), p('C')])  → 'B'
// Promise.any([fail(a), fail(b)]) → rejects AggregateError with all errors
```

The decision table (the interview answer):
- Every result required or fail together → all (API responses composing one screen)
- Partial failures acceptable, want the survivors → allSettled (batch uploads, analytics beacons)
- First result, or a timeout/cancel signal → race (fetch with deadline)
- First SUCCESS from redundant sources; all-fail is the only failure → any (multi-CDN, multi-region lookup)

The two traps worth naming: (1) with all, the non-failed promises KEEP RUNNING after one fails — all doesn't cancel anything, it just stops waiting; (2) all preserves input order in its output regardless of completion order — the results array maps to the input array, not the timing.
